__d(function(e,a,t,i,n,r,o){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Social Science \u2014 History",textbook:"Our Pasts-I",chapter_number:3,chapter_title:"In the Earliest Cities",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name any four major cities of the Harappan civilisation and their locations.",back:"Four major Harappan cities: (1) Harappa \u2014 Punjab region, present-day Pakistan; (2) Mohenjo-daro \u2014 Sindh, present-day Pakistan; (3) Dholavira \u2014 Gujarat, India; (4) Lothal \u2014 Gujarat, India (on a tributary of the Sabarmati river, known for its dockyard); (5) Kalibangan \u2014 Rajasthan, India. All were located along the Indus river system or in the wider Harappan cultural zone.",memory_hook:"HMD-LK = Harappa, Mohenjo-daro, Dholavira, Lothal, Kalibangan. 'History's Most Distinguished Locations Known' = the five Harappan stars!"},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Who discovered Mohenjo-daro and when?",back:"Mohenjo-daro was discovered by R.D. Banerji in 1922. The site was accidentally found when it was realised that ancient bricks at the location were of historical significance. Harappa had also been partially discovered earlier \u2014 bricks from the site were being used to build the Lahore-Multan railway in the 1850s, before archaeologists recognised its importance.",memory_hook:"R.D. BANERJI + 1922 = Mohenjo-daro discovery. Remember: 1922 \u2192 1+9+2+2 = 14 \u2192 B is the 2nd letter \u2192 Banerji! Or simply: 'B for Banerji, B for 1922's Big discovery!'"},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What was the 'citadel' in a Harappan city?",back:"The citadel was a raised, walled platform in Harappan cities. It was higher than the surrounding residential area and contained large, important public buildings \u2014 possibly used by rulers or priests for administration, religious activities, or storage. The Great Bath at Mohenjo-daro and the granary at Harappa are believed to be in the citadel area. It represents the seat of authority in a Harappan city.",memory_hook:"CITADEL = City's Top Administrative District, Elevated Level. The citadel was literally the 'top' of the city \u2014 raised physically and socially above the lower town. Think of it as the 'penthouse' of the ancient city!"},{id:"fc04",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the Great Bath and where is it found?",back:"The Great Bath is a large, well-built public bathing tank found in the citadel area of Mohenjo-daro. It is made of fine baked bricks with a watertight lining. Steps lead down into the tank from all sides. It was probably used for ritual bathing or public purification. The Great Bath is one of the most impressive and famous structures of the Harappan civilisation.",memory_hook:"GREAT BATH at MOHENJO-DARO = ancient India's first swimming pool! But it was probably used for ritual purification, not recreation. Mohenjo-daro also means 'Mound of the Dead' in Sindhi \u2014 a mysterious name for a mysterious civilisation!"},{id:"fc05",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What were Harappan seals? What were they used for?",back:"Harappan seals were small, generally square objects made of a soft stone called steatite (soapstone). They were engraved with animal figures (unicorn, bull, rhinoceros, tiger, elephant) and an undeciphered script. Seals were used to stamp bags or packets of goods being traded \u2014 like a brand name or quality mark identifying the origin and contents of a shipment. They were used by merchants and traders.",memory_hook:"SEAL = Stamping Every Article Leaving (the warehouse). Seals were ancient barcodes \u2014 stick one on your packet and everyone knows it's from you! The animal pictures were like company logos. And the script? Still a mystery \u2014 archaeology's unsolved puzzle!"},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"From where did the Harappans obtain their raw materials? Name the material and its source.",back:"Harappans obtained raw materials from far away: Copper \u2014 from present-day Rajasthan (India) and possibly Oman (West Asia); Tin \u2014 from Afghanistan and Iran; Gold \u2014 possibly from present-day Karnataka (India); Precious stones \u2014 from Gujarat, Iran, and Afghanistan. This extensive trade network shows that Harappan cities were part of a wide, interconnected ancient world.",memory_hook:"Copper=Rajasthan/Oman, Tin=Afghanistan/Iran, Gold=Karnataka, Stones=Gujarat/Iran/Afghanistan. Remember CT-GS: Copper-Tin-Gold-Stones and their sources. 'Can Talented Gold Seekers (find it all)?' = the Harappan trader's motto!"},{id:"fc07",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a 'grid pattern' of streets? Why were Harappan streets remarkable?",back:"A grid pattern is a street layout where roads cut each other at right angles, forming regular rectangular blocks \u2014 like a chessboard. Harappan streets were laid out in a grid pattern, which is remarkable for cities built 4700 years ago. This planned layout made navigation easier and allowed orderly placement of houses and drains. It is one of the world's earliest examples of planned urban street design.",memory_hook:"GRID = Great Roads Intersecting Deliberately. Picture a chessboard \u2014 that is a grid. Harappan streets were a giant chessboard 4700 years ago! Modern cities like Chandigarh use the same idea, inspired by the same principle."},{id:"fc08",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Describe the drainage system of Harappan cities.",back:"The Harappan drainage system worked as a hierarchy: (1) Individual houses had drains in bathrooms and kitchens; (2) These connected to covered street drains (covered with stone slabs); (3) Street drains connected to larger main drains running along major roads; (4) Inspection holes at intervals allowed workers to clean the drains. Houses, drains, and streets were all planned and built at the same time. It was one of the world's earliest underground sanitation systems.",memory_hook:"HOUSE DRAIN \u2192 STREET DRAIN \u2192 MAIN DRAIN = the three-level drain hierarchy. Think of it as a tree: leaves (house drains) \u2192 branches (street drains) \u2192 trunk (main drain). The whole 'tree' was covered and planned. Ancient India's first sewage system!"},{id:"fc09",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What was Lothal famous for?",back:"Lothal was a Harappan city located on the banks of a tributary of the Sabarmati river in present-day Gujarat. It was famous for two things: (1) It was an important centre for making stone, shell, and metal objects (crafts); (2) It is believed to have had a dockyard \u2014 a structure for loading, unloading, and repairing boats \u2014 suggesting it was a major port city involved in sea trade, possibly with Mesopotamia and other regions.",memory_hook:"LOTHAL = LOT of Trade and HALf water (coastal/riverine). Think: Lothal had a dockyard = boats = sea trade = Gujarat coast. Lothal was ancient India's first port city \u2014 the Mumbai of the Harappan world!"},{id:"fc10",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What were the three main groups of people in Harappan cities and what did each do?",back:"Three main groups: (1) Rulers \u2014 the people who planned the construction of special buildings, sent people to distant lands for raw materials, and maintained authority. (2) Scribes \u2014 literate people who knew how to write the undeciphered Harappan script; they helped prepare seals and possibly wrote on perishable materials. (3) Crafts persons \u2014 men and women who made all kinds of objects: tools, ornaments, pottery, seals, beads, and cloth.",memory_hook:"RSC = Rulers, Scribes, Crafts persons = the three pillars of Harappan city life. RSC = 'Running Sophisticated Cities' \u2014 each group had an essential role: rulers organised, scribes recorded, crafts persons produced!"},{id:"fc11",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Why does the uniformity of baked bricks across all Harappan cities suggest a strong central authority?",back:"All Harappan cities \u2014 separated by hundreds of kilometres \u2014 used bricks of the same proportions (the ratio of length:width:height was standardised). For this to happen without modern communication, there must have been a central authority that set and enforced building standards across all cities. This level of standardisation implies organised governance \u2014 someone with enough power and reach to ensure that builders in Harappa (Punjab) and Dholavira (Gujarat) were using the same brick sizes.",memory_hook:"SAME BRICKS EVERYWHERE = someone was in charge everywhere. Imagine if every house in India today used the exact same brick size \u2014 it would require a national standard and enforcement. The Harappans had this 4700 years ago!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Why was the Harappan trade network remarkable? How far did Harappan trade reach?",back:"The Harappan trade network was remarkable because it spanned thousands of kilometres. Raw materials came from: Rajasthan and Oman (copper), Afghanistan and Iran (tin), Karnataka (gold), and Gujarat/Iran/Afghanistan (precious stones). Harappan beads and seals have been found as far away as Mesopotamia (modern Iraq). This shows that the Harappan world was part of a vast, interconnected ancient trade network reaching from South Asia to West Asia \u2014 around 4700 years ago.",memory_hook:"HARAPPAN TRADE MAP: Rajasthan \u2192 Oman \u2192 Afghanistan \u2192 Iran \u2192 Karnataka \u2192 Gujarat = a trade network spanning 5000 km! Harappan merchants were the original global traders \u2014 before 'globalisation' was a word!"},{id:"fc13",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"What is 'faience' and what did the Harappans use it for?",back:"Faience is a special glazed material made by heating a mixture of powdered quartz (a mineral), water, and colourants. The resulting material is hard, shiny, and can be moulded into various shapes. Harappans used faience to make beads, bangles, and other decorative objects. Faience beads from Harappan cities have been found as far away as Mesopotamia (modern Iraq), showing they were traded over long distances. Faience represents a sophisticated knowledge of materials and heating techniques.",memory_hook:"FAIENCE = Fancy Artificial Imported-look Enamel-like Nice Ceramic Equivalent. Harappans created faience as a way to make beautiful, shiny objects without needing rare natural gemstones \u2014 ancient 'synthetic gems'!"},{id:"fc14",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Why has the Harappan script not been deciphered?",back:"The Harappan script remains undeciphered because: (1) There is no bilingual text (like the Rosetta Stone for Egyptian hieroglyphics) that shows the Harappan script alongside a known language \u2014 which was the key to deciphering Egyptian; (2) The script appears only on seals, with very short inscriptions \u2014 not enough text to find patterns; (3) We do not know what language family the script represents; (4) The script is not related to any known ancient script. Deciphering it remains one of archaeology's greatest challenges.",memory_hook:"UNDECIPHERED = no Rosetta Stone + short inscriptions + unknown language family. The Harappan script is history's most exclusive club \u2014 nobody's been able to crack the door open yet! One day, someone might find the ancient 'dictionary' that unlocks it."},{id:"fc15",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What crafts did Harappan people practise? Name five different materials they worked with.",back:"Harappan crafts persons worked with: (1) Stone \u2014 making tools and seals; (2) Shell \u2014 making ornaments and inlay work; (3) Copper and bronze \u2014 making tools, weapons, ornaments, and vessels; (4) Gold and silver \u2014 making fine ornaments and vessels; (5) Clay \u2014 making pottery (with black designs on red background) and terracotta toys and figurines; (6) Faience \u2014 making beads and bangles. They also wove cloth from cotton. Harappan crafts persons were highly specialised \u2014 some worked with only one material.",memory_hook:"SSCGCF = Stone, Shell, Copper, Gold, Clay, Faience = 'Super Skilled Crafts Give Cities Fame'. Add Cotton weaving and you have the complete Harappan craft industry!"},{id:"fc16",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Compare the drainage system of Harappan cities with sanitation in modern cities. What is similar? What is different?",back:"Similarities: Both use a hierarchy of drains (house \u2192 street \u2192 main); both are underground/covered; both are planned before/during construction; both require maintenance (inspection holes vs manholes). Differences: Modern systems use cement/PVC pipes rather than baked brick channels; modern systems include sewage treatment plants (Harappan waste just flowed into the ground or rivers); modern drainage is much larger scale. The basic principle \u2014 covered, hierarchical drainage connecting homes to main drains \u2014 is essentially the same after 4700 years.",memory_hook:"HARAPPAN DRAIN = ANCIENT VERSION of modern sewage. The principle hasn't changed in 4700 years: covered channels, hierarchy (small to big), maintenance access. The Harappans invented modern sanitation principles \u2014 we just scaled them up!"},{id:"fc17",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"What evidence suggests that Harappan cities had rulers with real authority?",back:"Several pieces of evidence suggest powerful rulers: (1) Uniform baked bricks across all cities \u2014 requiring city-wide (or civilisation-wide) enforcement of standards; (2) Planned, simultaneous construction of streets, drains, and buildings \u2014 requiring coordinated authority; (3) Large public buildings on the citadel \u2014 requiring organised labour and resources; (4) Long-distance trade expeditions \u2014 requiring organisation and resources; (5) Standardised weights and measures used across all cities \u2014 suggesting a centralised system. All of this points to rulers with real organisational power.",memory_hook:"RULER EVIDENCE = STANDARDS + PLANNING + BIG BUILDINGS + TRADE + WEIGHTS. 5 proofs of power! If everything in a city is standardised and planned, someone powerful was giving the orders."},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"What does the fact that Harappan objects (beads, seals) have been found in Mesopotamia tell us?",back:"Finding Harappan objects in Mesopotamia (modern Iraq) \u2014 thousands of kilometres from the Indus valley \u2014 tells us several important things: (1) Harappan merchants conducted long-distance sea or land trade with Mesopotamia; (2) Harappan goods (beads, possibly cloth) were valued in distant civilisations; (3) There was regular contact between the two earliest urban civilisations; (4) Mesopotamian texts actually mention 'Meluhha' \u2014 believed to be the Harappan region \u2014 as a trading partner. This reveals that the ancient world was far more connected than we often imagine.",memory_hook:"HARAPPAN BEADS IN MESOPOTAMIA = ancient globalisation! 4700 years ago, traders were carrying goods from Pakistan to Iraq. The ancient world had its own 'silk road' \u2014 and the Harappans were on it!"},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"What can the presence of private wells in Harappan houses tell us about Harappan society?",back:"Private wells in houses suggest: (1) Individual families had access to their own water supply \u2014 not entirely dependent on public sources; (2) This implies some degree of privacy and household autonomy; (3) The technology and skills to build wells existed at the household level, not just the city level; (4) It also suggests wealth differentiation \u2014 not every family could necessarily afford a private well, which may indicate social stratification. However, many houses without private wells could have used public wells that were also present in the lower town.",memory_hook:"PRIVATE WELL = private water = private property = social differentiation. Just as today, a house with its own water tank/borewell vs one depending on the municipal supply reflects economic differences. Harappan society was probably NOT perfectly equal!"},{id:"fc20",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Hard",front:"Why do historians say that 'none of the reasons can fully explain the end of all the cities' of the Harappan civilisation?",back:"This is because the decline was civilisation-wide \u2014 occurring across hundreds of sites spanning a vast area (modern Pakistan and northwest India). Each proposed reason has limitations: rivers drying up explains some sites (near the Saraswati/Ghaggar), but not all; deforestation was regional, not universal; floods explain Mohenjo-daro but not inland cities; rulers losing control doesn't explain WHY they lost control. A single cause cannot produce uniform collapse across such a large, diverse geographic area. The most likely explanation is a combination of multiple factors that reinforced each other \u2014 an ancient example of systemic collapse.",memory_hook:"NO SINGLE CAUSE = complex systems fail in complex ways. Like how a car doesn't stop just because of one thing \u2014 the engine fails AND the battery dies AND the fuel runs out. Harappan collapse = ancient civilisational 'multi-system failure'!"}]}},2913,[]);